## Rungs 4 to 7: specified, read, guarded, shipped

| Rung | Variant | Upstream areas |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 4 Proven | `rung-4-proven` | `tests/`, `tests/bdd/features`, `guides/testing`, `stryker.config.json` |
| 5 Reader | `rung-5-reader` | `apps/reader`, `packages/svelte/ui-*`, `packages/svelte/course` |
| 6 Guarded | `rung-6-guarded` | `packages/jsr/identity`, `packages/svelte/utils/rbac`, `packages/svelte/ui-primitives/src/utils/sanitize.ts`, `SECURITY.md` |
| 7 Shipped | `rung-7-shipped` | `.github/workflows`, `release/`, `Dockerfile`, tutors-release-harness |

Each variant's `RUNG.md` lists the exact files. The reading note has the full map.

---

## Supabase: the data behind signed-in use

- `supabase/migrations/`: nine SQL migrations at the pinned commit
- `guides/MIGRATIONS.md`: expand and contract, so a rolling release never breaks the version still running;
  "a merged migration is never edited, deleted or renamed"
- `pnpm check:migrations` checks a new migration before CI does
- Browser code reaches personal data only through `@tutors/data-api` (`packages/svelte/data-api`);
  `tests/architecture/browser-data-access.test.ts` holds that line

In anonymous mode (`PUBLIC_ANON_MODE=TRUE` in `.env.example`) none of this runs. That is why the quick start
needs no backend.

---

## Realtime and live

- `packages/jsr/realtime` (`@tutors/realtime`): the realtime **port**
- `packages/svelte/realtime-supabase`: the one **adapter** that drives Supabase Realtime channels
- `apps/live`: the live classroom
- `tests/architecture/realtime-boundary.test.ts` fails if any other file opens a channel

~~~ts
const ALLOWED = ["packages/svelte/realtime-supabase/src/index.ts",
                 "apps/reader/static/excalidraw-editor.html"];
~~~

This is rung 3's ports and adapters, at full size.

---

## Catalogue, time and the validator

| App | What it is | What it shares with the ladder |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `apps/catalogue` | course catalogue and discovery | lists courses; its data comes from the backend |
| `apps/time` | student activity and time, using `packages/jsr/time` | a JSR library, like rung 1's model |
| `apps/validator` | checks a published `tutors.json` (rule V020) or a local course archive | the same `course-rules` behind `check-course.sh` since week 1 |

In anonymous mode the catalogue, live and time apps start but show no courses or students. CONTRIBUTING.md:
"An empty page is expected, not a broken setup."

---

## JSR publishing

- `.github/workflows/jsr-publish.yml`: when a final release is published, every library in `packages/jsr`
  whose `deno.json` version JSR does not serve yet is published, dependencies first (Rule 0775)
- `deno publish` authenticates with GitHub's OIDC token, so no JSR token is stored, and JSR records Sigstore
  provenance
- That is how `tutors-create validate`, which every course's standard `netlify.toml` runs, reaches lecturers

Your course in this module is validated by code that travelled this route.

---

## The release harness

A separate repository, tutors-sdk/tutors-release-harness (pinned at 1.63.0):

> Run the same scripted traffic through two identical stacks that differ only in the application image,
> normalise the noise, diff everything observable, and fail unless each diff hunk is claimed by a changelog
> entry or a Rule.

- it verifies the cosign signature of every image it pulls from `quay.io/tutors-sdk/tutors-<app>`
- being separate, it "cannot be quietly weakened by the PR it is judging" (its README)

Rung 7's claims were promises that tests keep. Upstream's claims are differences that a harness matches.

---

## The release itself: standard work

`release/SOP.md`: three roles, twelve steps, "one owner per step, and a done-when that is a file or a recorded
action, never a feeling".

| Lean idea | Where it lives |
| --- | --- |
| Jidoka (stop the line) | the harness gate; `release/claims.yaml` |
| Standard work | the SOP itself |
| Gemba (go and look) | the reviewer's glance, step 8 |
| Kaizen | a stop opens a 5 Whys that ends in one countermeasure |

Contributors own steps 3 and 4: the changelog line with its artefact hints, and the claims.

---

## Service level objectives

- `release/slo.yaml`: indicative availability and latency targets per app, over a 28 day window
- The reader is strictest: `reader-availability` has target `0.995`, "about 3h22m of errors in 28 days"
- Grafana alerts are generated from the file (`pnpm slo:alerts`); `pnpm check:slo` fails if they drift: a
  Closure Check, like rung 4's audit
- The harness keeps its own floors (`slo/floors.yaml`) and "judges the stricter of the two, so lowering a
  number here never loosens a release"

Rung 7's budgets were build time and site size. These are promises about a running service.

---

## Supply chain: the OpenSSF Scorecard and friends

- `.github/workflows/scorecard.yml`: weekly (`cron: "20 7 * * 1"`, Mondays 07:20 UTC) and on branch
  protection changes
- The README carries the Scorecard and OpenSSF Baseline badges; `docs/security/scorecard-baseline.md` records
  the baseline
- `codeql.yml` scans the code; `zizmor.yml` audits the workflows themselves
- `image-build.yml` signs each image with cosign, keyless, from GitHub OIDC

Rung 6 began this story with pinned dependencies. The real repository finishes it.

---

## Sizes, side by side

| | Rung 7 | tutors-mono-repo |
| --- | --- | --- |
| apps | a cli and a reader | 5 (`reader`, `catalogue`, `live`, `time`, `validator`) |
| packages | a handful of `@ladder/*` | 11 under `packages/jsr`, 14 under `packages/svelte` (`utils` holds 8 more) |
| workflows | one template | 17 |
| EARS Rules | a few dozen `R-NNN` ids | 441 Rules in 85 feature files |
| Node | 22.18 or later | 26.13 or later |

You will not hold all of it in your head. You do not need to. You need the rung your change sits on.

---

## What the ladder left out, on purpose

Every `RUNG.md` has a "Deliberately left out" section. Read together, they are the list of what is new at the
Summit:

- state that outlives a page: accounts, enrolment, analytics, presence
- more than one app, and the shared packages that make that bearable
- operation: images, Kubernetes overlays in `deploy/`, dashboards in `observability/`, SLOs, a harness

None of it changes the centre. A course folder still becomes `tutors.json`, and a reader still renders it.

---

## Summary

- Every rung has a home in the real repository, and you know its shape
- What is new at the Summit is mostly **scale** and **operation**: data, realtime, releases, SLOs
- The same patterns hold it together: ports, Closure Checks, Ratchets, one gate, a Human Boundary
- Next talk: how to change it safely, with an agent, one small improvement at a time
